    {
      "id": "f9lldev",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Matter"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Matter exists in five fundamental states: solid, liquid, gas, plasma (ionized gas), and Bose-Einstein Condensate (BEC).",
        "Water reaches its maximum density and minimum volume at 4°C, causing ice (density 0.92 g/cm³) to float.",
        "Steel is scientifically more elastic than rubber because it requires a larger deforming force to produce the same strain.",
        "Latent heat represents energy absorbed or released during a phase change without altering temperature.",
        "Viscosity of liquids decreases with rising temperature, whereas viscosity of gases increases with rising temperature."
      ],
      "id": "2wkshhr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "naypa3m",
      "content": "• Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x4eo7b5",
      "content": "• It is made up of tiny particles called atoms and molecules."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ieod0hc",
      "content": "• Matter exists in different forms in nature."
    },
    {
      "id": "1udhwf5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. States of Matter"
    },
    {
      "id": "8lq69ye",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>State</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Properties</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Examples</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Solid</p>",
            "<p>Fixed shape and volume</p>",
            "<p>Iron, wood, ice</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Liquid</p>",
            "<p>Fixed volume but no fixed shape</p>",
            "<p>Water, oil, milk</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Gas</p>",
            "<p>No fixed shape or volume</p>",
            "<p>Air, oxygen, carbon dioxide</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "csb1iyv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Physical Properties of Matter"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0s3mjvy",
      "content": "• Mass – amount of matter in a substance."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "luhvsq5",
      "content": "• Volume – space occupied by matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Water has maximum density at 4°C (1000 kg/m³ or 1 g/cm³).",
      "id": "igs95ok"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5nvncwo",
      "content": "• Density – mass per unit volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gn3bgpk",
      "content": "• Elasticity – ability of a material to regain its original shape."
    },
    {
      "id": "ap0bzr2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Changes in States of Matter"
    },
    {
      "id": "5cnh86k",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Process</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Melting</p>",
            "<p>Solid changes into liquid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Freezing</p>",
            "<p>Liquid changes into solid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Evaporation</p>",
            "<p>Liquid changes into gas</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Condensation</p>",
            "<p>Gas changes into liquid</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sublimation</p>",
            "<p>Solid changes directly into gas</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jpg0lft",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Density"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w7n5jmn",
      "content": "Density is defined as mass per unit volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Property",
        "Mass",
        "Weight"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Definition",
          "Amount of matter present in a body",
          "Gravitational force with which Earth attracts a body"
        ],
        [
          "Constancy",
          "Constant everywhere in the universe",
          "Varies from place to place with gravity (g)"
        ],
        [
          "Formula & SI Unit",
          "Measured in kilograms (kg)",
          "W = m × g, measured in Newtons (N)"
        ],
        [
          "Zero Value",
          "Can never be zero",
          "Can be zero where gravity is zero (e.g., center of Earth)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "u3szohx"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "26clt1b",
      "content": "Formula: Density = Mass / Volume"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fhdyupx",
      "content": "SI Unit of Density = kg/m³"
    },
    {
      "id": "og85t8r",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Elasticity"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f7b0jsw",
      "content": "Elasticity is the property of a material to regain its original shape after deformation when the external force is removed."
    },
    {
      "id": "zx3dlmt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Pressure"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ymt8kvh",
      "content": "Pressure is defined as force acting per unit area."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tpto5vd",
      "content": "Formula: Pressure = Force / Area"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "y4rc2wu",
      "content": "SI Unit = Pascal (Pa)"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Barometer Trends: A sudden drop indicates a storm/cyclone, a slow drop indicates rain, and a slow rise indicates clear weather.",
        "Sublimation Substances: Camphor, Ammonium Chloride, Dry Ice (solid CO2), and Naphthalene directly change from solid to gas.",
        "Pascal's Law: Explains the working principle behind hydraulic lifts, hydraulic presses, and hydraulic brakes.",
        "Surface Tension Effects: Causes raindrops to be spherical, allows insects to walk on water, and enables capillary action."
      ],
      "id": "5us808e"
    },
    {
      "id": "dr2ybq0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "Important Facts"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rh932ws",
      "content": "• Matter has mass and occupies space."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pcdt9w5",
      "content": "• Density = Mass / Volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "07j63p7",
      "content": "• SI unit of pressure is Pascal."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u60zr6v",
      "content": "• There are three main states of matter: solid, liquid and gas."
    }
  ],
